---
import { featured, secondary, compact } from '../data/projects';
import { confusion as cm } from '../data/detector';
const [forti,soc] = secondary;
---
<section class="work-section wrap" id="work" aria-labelledby="work-title">
  <header class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Source, approach, evidence</p><h2 id="work-title">Selected work.</h2></div><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/Yassine-El-Ghazi?tab=repositories">All repositories <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></header>
  <article class="case-feature" id="p-android" aria-labelledby="android-title">
    <div class="case-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow case-category">Android Malware Detection</p>
      <h3 id="android-title">Reading an APK<br /> before it runs.</h3>
      <p class="case-intro">A static first pass at a difficult question: what can an Android package tell us about its behavior without executing it?</p>
      <dl class="case-facts">
        <div><dt>Implementation</dt><dd>A Python CLI scanner and Flask interface. Androguard extracts manifest permissions and intent actions; a Random Forest returns a classification, confidence, and high-risk permission flags.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Approach</dt><dd>Five classifiers compared on the same split. A 200-tree Random Forest was retained for its interpretable feature importances.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Limits</dt><dd>Obfuscation and dynamic payloads can evade the static scan. Its manifest-only extractor reads a subset of the full Drebin feature vocabulary.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <a class="text-link" href={featured.url}>Read the code &amp; report <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      <ul class="technologies" aria-label="Android project technologies">{featured.tags.map(t=><li>{t}</li>)}</ul>
    </div>
    <div class="case-evidence">
      <figure class="analysis-figure">
        <figcaption><span class="eyebrow">Inside the scanner</span><span class="figure-note">Static · no execution</span></figcaption>
        <div class="apk-input"><span class="serif">APK</span><p>Manifest permissions<br />and intent actions</p></div>
        <div class="flow-connector" aria-hidden="true">↓</div>
        <div class="feature-vector"><strong>215</strong><div><b>Drebin feature vocabulary</b><p>Manifest signals mapped<br />into a binary vector.</p></div></div>
        <div class="flow-connector" aria-hidden="true">↓</div>
        <div class="classifier"><span class="serif">Random Forest</span><p>200 trees · interpretable importances</p></div>
        <div class="verdict-line"><span>Classification</span><span>Confidence</span><span>Risk flags</span></div>
        <p class="figure-caption">The data path implemented by the public scanner. The package stays unexecuted throughout.</p>
      </figure>
      <p class="result-summary"><strong>98.7% held-out dataset accuracy.</strong> Full Drebin-215 benchmark; the manifest-only scanner reads fewer signals, so its results can differ.</p>
      <details class="evaluation">
        <summary>Inspect the evaluation <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
        <p><strong>98.7% accuracy</strong> on the full Drebin-215 dataset’s 20% held-out split. The report gives 98.74%; the documented fixed-seed re-run gives 98.77%.</p>
        <div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Held-out results · {(cm.benign+cm.malicious).toLocaleString('en-US')} apps</caption><thead><tr><td></td><th scope="col">Predicted benign</th><th scope="col">Predicted malicious</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Actual benign</th><td>{cm.tn.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td><td>{cm.fp}</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Actual malicious</th><td>{cm.fn}</td><td>{cm.tp.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
        <p class="evidence-caveat">The benchmark includes code-level Drebin features that the scanner’s manifest-only extractor cannot read. These dataset results describe the trained model; scanner performance can differ.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </article>
  <article class="case-network" id="p-forti" aria-labelledby="forti-title">
    <div class="network-intro"><p class="eyebrow">FortiGate VPN &amp; HA Lab</p><h3 id="forti-title">Two sites.<br /><em>One secure link.</em></h3><p>{forti.description}</p><a class="text-link" href={forti.url}>Explore the FortiGate lab <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
    <div class="network-evidence"><figure class="network-diagram"><figcaption class="eyebrow">FortiGate VPN &amp; high availability</figcaption><div class="network-topology"><div class="network-site"><span class="figure-note">Headquarters</span><strong>Active</strong><span class="ha-link">↕ HA heartbeat</span><strong class="passive">Passive</strong></div><div class="network-tunnel"><span>IKEv2 / IPsec</span><i aria-hidden="true"></i><small>ESP on the WAN</small></div><div class="network-site remote"><span class="figure-note">Branch</span><strong>FortiGate</strong><span class="figure-note">Dynamic address</span></div></div><p class="figure-caption">EVE-NG · Active-passive pair at headquarters with an encrypted tunnel to the branch.</p></figure>
    <div class="network-outcomes"><div><h4>Validated</h4><p>Bidirectional traffic, interactive RDP, ESP packet captures, and traffic recovery after HA failover.</p></div><div><h4>Investigated</h4><p>A return-path routing issue, plus documented production-hardening gaps.</p></div></div>
    <details class="evaluation"><summary>Read the validation log <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><dl class="checks">{forti.table?.rows.map(([k,v])=><div><dt>{k}</dt><dd>{v}</dd></div>)}</dl></details></div>
  </article>
  <div class="supporting-work">
    <article class="case-soc" id="p-soc" aria-labelledby="soc-title"><p class="eyebrow">SecureBank SOC Lab · Team project</p><h3 id="soc-title">From server logs<br /> to useful signals.</h3><p>{soc.description}</p><figure class="soc-flow"><figcaption class="sr-only">SOC data flow</figcaption><span>Web logs</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span>Wazuh</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span>Elastic / Kibana</span></figure><p class="contribution"><strong>My contribution.</strong> Helper scripts for service health, investigation, and recovery checks.</p><p class="case-limit">A virtual SOC lab; production detection coverage and response metrics have not been established.</p><a class="text-link" href={soc.url}>Read the SecureBank SOC Lab <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article>
    <article class="case-linux" id="p-rice" aria-labelledby="rice-title"><p class="eyebrow">MyHyprlandRice · Linux</p><h3 id="rice-title">A desktop,<br /><em>made repeatable.</em></h3><p>{compact.description}</p><dl class="state-diagram"><div><dt>dotfiles/</dt><dd>Declarative configuration</dd></div><div><dt>defaults/</dt><dd>First-run mutable state</dd></div><div><dt>Recovery tooling</dt><dd>Reversible changes</dd></div></dl><p class="case-limit">A personal Linux setup with host-specific configuration requirements.</p><a class="text-link" href={compact.url}>Explore MyHyprlandRice <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article>
  </div>
</section>
